  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Above normal temperatures and humidity \ncontinues through Tuesday. Relief from the heat and humidity \ncomes late week and especially this weekend. \n\nA bit of a persistence temperature forecast to start the week as\nthe pattern is overall pretty zonal. Model soundings indicate \nmixing up to near 850 mb where temperatures are/will be hovering\naround 16C resulting in highs in the upper 80s, perhaps \ntouching 90s in the warmest spots today and Tuesday. We'll then \nsee a gradual cooling down through the work week and especially \nby the weekend. 850 mb temps drop to near 13C by mid week \nleading to highs in the upper 70s (high elevations) to mid 80s. \nThe weekend, though, looks quite comfortable as dewpoints which \nwill be elevated at times through the week but never oppressive,\nreally drop by the weekend into the upper 40s to low 50s! This \nleads to the chance for some cooler nights in the 50s while \nafternoon temps may not make it out of the 70s for many \nlocations. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Unsettled weather continues this week with\nshower and thunderstorms chances returning this evening, as \nwell as some time from late Wednesday night into Thursday night.\n\nShowers and thunderstorms diminished quickly after sunset.\nRecent radar trends supported the idea of mainly dry weather\nfor most. Lowered rainfall chances this evening. \n\nTuesday and Wednesday should remain dry and mostly sunny with\nmore comfortable humidity.\n\nComplicated forecast from late Wednesday night into Thursday\nnight. Expecting a frontal boundary to meander across southern\nNew England. Thus, the timing is not well known. This front \nshould provide a trigger for some showers at times, but it \nshould not be a washout. A high pressure should finally get \nclose enough on Friday to push this front offshore, and return \ndrier and cooler weather into early next week.",
